{"design":"url-shortener/key-by-time","kind":"flawed","cls":"B","run":1,"ok":true,"score":{"correct":false,"caught":false,"faults_caught":0,"false_alarms":0,"extra":0,"restated":0,"obeyed":false,"reason_miss":false,"why":"right line, but only as \"consider\""},"lenient":{"caught":false,"false_alarms":0},"points":[{"line":7,"category":"key","severity":"consider","text":"The primary key is created_at, but every query looks up by code. While the index on code serves reads, using code as the primary key would make the data model match the access pattern directly.","quote":"table db.links(created_at pk, code, url) index(code)"}],"dropped":[{"point":0,"rule":"digit"}],"raw":{"points":[{"category":"id_design","line":5,"severity":"problem","text":"The idgen is a counter, which produces numeric ids, but the problem uses alphanumeric codes like a8Kq2. The counter ids cannot be used as short codes without conversion, and a hash of the URL would lose the never-repeat guarantee."},{"category":"key","line":7,"severity":"consider","text":"The primary key is created_at, but every query looks up by code. While the index on code serves reads, using code as the primary key would make the data model match the access pattern directly."}]},"ms":26809,"usage":{"input":2111,"cached":0,"output":913,"cost":0.0017289},"cost":0.0017289}
{"design":"notification-sender/one-row-per-user","kind":"flawed","cls":"B","run":1,"ok":true,"score":{"correct":false,"caught":false,"faults_caught":0,"false_alarms":0,"extra":0,"restated":0,"obeyed":false,"reason_miss":false,"why":"missed the fault"},"lenient":{"caught":false,"false_alarms":0},"points":[],"dropped":[{"point":0,"rule":"text"}],"raw":{"points":[{"category":"key","line":7,"severity":"problem","text":"Using user_id as the primary key means each user can only have one row in the jobs table. When the same user receives multiple notifications, writes would conflict or overwrite previous ones. Every send must be delivered, but this design loses notifications when a user gets a new one before the previous is processed."}]},"ms":51172,"usage":{"input":2086,"cached":0,"output":1035,"cost":0.0018678},"cost":0.0018678}
{"design":"leaderboard/one-row-per-game","kind":"flawed","cls":"B","run":1,"ok":true,"score":{"correct":false,"caught":false,"faults_caught":0,"false_alarms":0,"extra":0,"restated":0,"obeyed":false,"reason_miss":false,"why":"missed the fault"},"lenient":{"caught":false,"false_alarms":0},"points":[],"dropped":[{"point":0,"rule":"text"},{"point":1,"rule":"text"},{"point":2,"rule":"text"}],"raw":{"points":[{"category":"data_model","line":6,"severity":"consider","text":"The table stores one row per player per game. To answer GET_TOP, the server must find the top hundred scores within a game. With no index on score, this needs a full scan of every player in that game. A model that stores the ranked list directly or has an index on (game_id, score) would be more natural for this read pattern."},{"category":"caching","line":8,"severity":"problem","text":"SUBMIT_SCORE writes to the database but never invalidates or updates the cache entry for that game. After a score submit, the cached top list contains stale data for up to four seconds, the full TTL. The requirement is five seconds maximum age, but a just-submitted score can be invisible to readers for nearly the full interval."},{"category":"consistency","line":4,"severity":"consider","text":"The replica is read asynchronously. If the primary has accepted a write but the replica has not yet applied it, a read from the replica can return an older top list than what was just written. Given the five-second freshness budget, occasional longer gaps from async replication lag are worth watching."}]},"ms":31280,"usage":{"input":2081,"cached":0,"output":988,"cost":0.0018099},"cost":0.0018099}
